  "note": "What every function outside the module can do when module code calls it, for tools/testshape. A package's default covers every function and method of it; an entry under functions overrides it, keyed Name, Type (all its methods), Type.Method, Type.Field (a spawner field), or init (the package's initializer, overriding its module's). Every module the measured builds link a package of has an entry under modules, whose init classifies its packages' initializers, which a binary's setup runs; a package's default does not apply to its initializer. Every initializer those modules link was read when its entry was written: each only sets variables (tables, registrations, the environment), or reads the clock once to record the process's start time, which like the environment is the process's setting, not a touch of a test. A key ending /... covers every package under that path that has no entry of its own. Classes: none (touch-free); a comma list of store, git, proc, net, fs, clock; dispatching (it calls back what was registered with it or handed to it), alone or in the list; calls:Interface (it calls back the methods of Interface, named importpath.Name or error, on the values handed to it that implement it, each outside type's method charged by its own class), in the list; spawn:N (argument N is the program it runs); spawn:field. Where it is unsure whether a function touches, it is classified as touching: every approximation errs away from unit. A callback handed to an outside function needs no dispatching class: handing a function value to outside code is a call of every address-taken function of its signature. An interface method is keyed Interface.Method in the package that declares the interface; a call module code makes through an outside interface is charged by that entry. A handle's touch (a file, a connection) is charged where it is acquired, so the reads and writes through io's interfaces are none.",
